1
00:00:03,479 --> 00:00:09,000
My state of mind regarding the pilfering from which I had been so unexpectedly exonerated

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,960
did not impel me to frank disclosure; but I hope it had some dregs of good at the bottom of it.

3
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:20,560
I do not recall that I felt any tenderness of conscience in reference to Mrs. Joe,

4
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:26,600
when the fear of being found out was lifted off me. But I loved Joe,—perhaps for no better reason

5
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:32,600
in those early days than because the dear fellow let me love him,—and, as to him, my inner self was

6
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,720
not so easily composed. It was much upon my mind (particularly when I first saw him looking about

7
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:45,600
for his file) that I ought to tell Joe the whole truth. Yet I did not, and for the reason that I

8
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:53,560
mistrusted that if I did, he would think me worse than I was. The fear of losing Joe’s confidence,

9
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:58,159
and of thenceforth sitting in the chimney corner at night staring drearily at my

10
00:00:58,159 --> 00:01:05,039
forever lost companion and friend, tied up my tongue. I morbidly represented to myself that

11
00:01:05,039 --> 00:01:09,840
if Joe knew it, I never afterwards could see him at the fireside feeling his fair whisker,

12
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:15,239
without thinking that he was meditating on it. That, if Joe knew it, I never afterwards could

13
00:01:15,239 --> 00:01:21,560
see him glance, however casually, at yesterday’s meat or pudding when it came on to-day’s table,

14
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:28,560
without thinking that he was debating whether I had been in the pantry. That, if Joe knew it,

15
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:34,920
and at any subsequent period of our joint domestic life remarked that his beer was flat or thick,

16
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:41,680
the conviction that he suspected tar in it, would bring a rush of blood to my face. In a word,

17
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:46,039
I was too cowardly to do what I knew to be right, as I had been too cowardly

18
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:53,439
to avoid doing what I knew to be wrong. I had had no intercourse with the world at that time,

19
00:01:53,439 --> 00:02:00,159
and I imitated none of its many inhabitants who act in this manner. Quite an untaught genius,

20
00:02:00,159 --> 00:02:04,319
I made the discovery of the line of action for myself.

21
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:08,960
As I was sleepy before we were far away from the prison-ship, Joe took me on his back again

22
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:15,520
and carried me home. He must have had a tiresome journey of it, for Mr. Wopsle, being knocked up,

23
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,159
was in such a very bad temper that if the Church had been thrown open,

24
00:02:19,159 --> 00:02:25,399
he would probably have excommunicated the whole expedition, beginning with Joe and myself. In

25
00:02:25,399 --> 00:02:30,840
his lay capacity, he persisted in sitting down in the damp to such an insane extent,

26
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:34,159
that when his coat was taken off to be dried at the kitchen fire,

27
00:02:34,159 --> 00:02:40,600
the circumstantial evidence on his trousers would have hanged him, if it had been a capital offence.

28
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:44,920
By that time, I was staggering on the kitchen floor like a little drunkard,

29
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:49,960
through having been newly set upon my feet, and through having been fast asleep,

30
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:57,239
and through waking in the heat and lights and noise of tongues. As I came to myself (with

31
00:02:57,239 --> 00:03:03,600
the aid of a heavy thump between the shoulders, and the restorative exclamation “Yah! Was there

32
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:10,199
ever such a boy as this!” from my sister,) I found Joe telling them about the convict’s confession,

33
00:03:10,199 --> 00:03:16,439
and all the visitors suggesting different ways by which he had got into the pantry. Mr. Pumblechook

34
00:03:16,439 --> 00:03:22,439
made out, after carefully surveying the premises, that he had first got upon the roof of the forge,

35
00:03:22,439 --> 00:03:27,920
and had then got upon the roof of the house, and had then let himself down the kitchen chimney by

36
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:33,039
a rope made of his bedding cut into strips; and as Mr. Pumblechook was very positive and

37
00:03:33,039 --> 00:03:40,439
drove his own chaise-cart—over everybody—it was agreed that it must be so. Mr. Wopsle, indeed,

38
00:03:40,439 --> 00:03:47,399
wildly cried out, “No!” with the feeble malice of a tired man; but, as he had no theory, and no

39
00:03:47,399 --> 00:03:53,880
coat on, he was unanimously set at naught,—not to mention his smoking hard behind, as he stood with

40
00:03:53,880 --> 00:04:00,319
his back to the kitchen fire to draw the damp out: which was not calculated to inspire confidence.

41
00:04:00,319 --> 00:04:04,319
This was all I heard that night before my sister clutched me, as a slumberous offence

42
00:04:04,319 --> 00:04:09,079
to the company’s eyesight, and assisted me up to bed with such a strong hand that I seemed to

43
00:04:09,079 --> 00:04:14,800
have fifty boots on, and to be dangling them all against the edges of the stairs. My state of mind,

44
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:18,800
as I have described it, began before I was up in the morning, and lasted long after the

45
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:24,000
subject had died out, and had ceased to be mentioned saving on exceptional occasions.
